<TopicPage slug="subjects/music/intermediate/reading-notation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Musical notation is <strong>a thousand-year-old data format for sound</strong> — pitch on one
    axis, time on the other, instructions layered on top. Like any encoding it loses information
    (no notation captures a performer fully) and encodes cultural assumptions (it handles Western
    rhythms far better than Indian tala or African polyrhythm). Reading it is reading music's
    engineering drawings.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the grid, the fractions, the accidentals, the expression" widget="equationStep" config={notationCfg} />

  <h2>Notation in perspective</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Guido d'Arezzo (~1025) invented the staff</strong> — and claimed it cut learning time from ten years to two. Notation is educational technology as much as preservation.</li>
    <li><strong>It enabled composition as we know it:</strong> complex polyphony (Bach) requires writing parts for independent voices. The notation made the repertoire possible, not just recorded it.</li>
    <li><strong>Alternatives exist:</strong> tablature (guitar), shape notes, piano roll (player pianos → DAWs), Indian sargam, graphic scores (Stockhausen). The DAW timeline — rectangles on a grid — is this century's dominant notation, and it's graphical again.</li>
    <li><strong>Notation is conservative by design:</strong> it fixes what a performer may not change and leaves the rest to interpretation. The gaps between the symbols are where style lives.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Guido of Arezzo, Micrologus (~1025); Gould, Behind Bars; standard theory texts (AP Music Theory).</p>
</TopicPage>
